    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Djenné grew from the older urban settlement of Djenné-Djeno, first occupied around 200 BCE, and the present town, settled after 1000 CE, took over as the older site was abandoned by about 1400.</li><li>Between the 15th and 17th centuries much of the trans-Saharan trade in salt, gold and slaves that passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> also passed through Djenné, and both towns were centres of Islamic scholarship.</li><li>Sonni Ali took the town in 1471, Moroccan forces held the region after 1591, and later it passed to the Segou kingdom, the Massina Empire, the Toucouleur Empire and, in 1893, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Each year the floods of the Bani and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> rivers turn Djenné into an island, reached by causeways. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> houses must be replastered at least every other year, and the Great Mosque every year.</p></aside>
    </section>

